#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "c3aac9bd-c462-5664-a196-278a9671fbbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4243" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{-2}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{n-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{n-3} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {8}^{n-3} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({8}^{n-3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "4af09b88-5f57-572f-ae64-bfe64e94450d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4244" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16}  = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,1 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac { \\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "154a5006-6c4f-58c7-a228-ccbefabd5050" :question/math-academy-id "e-4245" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54,$ and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and  $r= 3,$ we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1}\\[3pt]\n\t\ta_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1}\\[3pt]\n\t54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3\\[3pt]\n\t54&=27a_1\\[3pt]\n\ta_1&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dab19498-0f30-5fbf-b741-364f1b7f05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dab19498-0f30-5fbf-b741-364f1b7f05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293779} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b7f67d-5586-52c4-a88d-588629b42516" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29e6ffa8-9f33-56f5-810e-d7c6edc1eff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-277931" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eebd01-d696-5583-866b-bc7ee0975ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $42$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324275} {:answer/id #uuid "5efa3e05-6fbd-561f-aad6-9039ff65a874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $39$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324765} {:answer/id #uuid "869369c2-c60a-5bfe-9e90-d7fc384dd4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $38$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325115} {:answer/id #uuid "e425578a-3944-5da8-bbf6-a67efada93ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $41$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325979} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012} :answer-field/id #uuid "1793d83a-a4d4-5328-b990-9f3e282f524a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5d1d92f-ad9b-5aee-bc72-594097ea04d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39648" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31f79c15-e015-58ee-9759-1651faf25e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333447} {:answer/id #uuid "398d4c7a-0785-51dd-a6f3-ad5515ef3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333527} {:answer/id #uuid "45ca5716-9eb2-586f-a070-fc824e9589a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333633} {:answer/id #uuid "56d76ff2-b484-54dd-a935-e81f1b63a229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333775} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e570b2-ef64-5bdf-9d62-b47beca6903d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398d4c7a-0785-51dd-a6f3-ad5515ef3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333527} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85b7624-09f8-51bb-bb59-2a37f079db87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5456760-9cdb-54e3-82ce-b6d6e704f8ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-67516" :question/problem "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $3$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$907?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=3$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 3 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-4}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $907.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-5} & >907 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(3)}^{n-5} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ (n-5)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ n-5 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5≈11.199\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $907.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68a45c-7e16-5fb2-afee-3dfbb5fdb55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $9$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357841} {:answer/id #uuid "42a3c94b-95c3-5072-8e13-4256db80a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $7$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358320} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a780c1-259a-5ea0-bd30-a23c589220b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359160} {:answer/id #uuid "caa963c4-4072-5bbe-b660-3ffaf8cff6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $6$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359530} {:answer/id #uuid "f588cff2-7042-54f7-9627-96d8e9d8f0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f588cff2-7042-54f7-9627-96d8e9d8f0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359928} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90aa030-babb-5358-a2e1-4ec64e1f8392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95917747-a74b-509e-9924-fa8c1433f09e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9743" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,28,\\,196,\\,1\\,372,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{28}{4}=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {7}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 4\\cdot {7}^{n-1} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {7}^{n-1} & >500\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1≈7.744\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}]}
